Article 8
(1) Regional Government conducts prevention of acts of violence against women and children by means of:
a. conducting socialization of laws and regulations;
b. providing counseling/guidance;
c. providing education on the dangers of domestic violence;
d. conducting seminars/workshops or the like;
e. forming work networks in efforts to prevent violence;
f. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
g. forming a violence prevention system, mapping of locations or areas prone to violence occurrence; and
h. increasing public awareness in behaving in accordance with religious norms.
(2) In addition to conducting prevention as referred to in paragraph (1), prevention of acts of violence against children is also conducted by means of increasing family capacity in child protection and child care skills.
(3) Apart from the Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is obligated to be implemented by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. educational institutions;
d. health institutions;
e. social community institutions;
f. Desa pakraman; and
g. business sector and other institutions.

Article 11
(1) The Regional Government implements integrated services for victims of acts of violence through the following activities:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. counseling;
d. spiritual guidance;
e. social rehabilitation services;
f. legal aid and legal assistance services; and
g. repatriation and reintegration.
(2) The services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of:
a. special service rooms within the police force;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and safe houses; and/or
d. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for victim recovery.
(3) The services as referred to in paragraph (1), including the preparation of visum et repertum, are provided free of charge.
(4) The provision of services to victims of acts of violence is carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government together with P2TP2A and other social institutions;
(5) P2TP2A and other social institutions as referred to in paragraph (4) may receive and send case referrals from other service units through a network;
(6) Integrated services for victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), paragraph (3), and paragraph (4) are implemented in accordance with Standard Operating Procedures;
(7) Provisions regarding the Standard Operating Procedures as referred to in paragraph (6) are stipulated by a Regent Regulation.

Article 15
(1) The Regional Government, in the context of providing services to victims of violence against women and children, establishes P2TP2A;
(2) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) functions as: a. an information center for women and children;
b. a service center for women and children victims of violence; and c. an empowerment center for women and children.
(3) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) is obligated to: a. provide services as quickly as possible and free of charge to witnesses and/or victims;
b. provide protection and fulfillment of the rights of witnesses and/or victims to health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, return, social reintegration and legal assistance;
c. cooperate with certain institutions in providing translators and volunteer assistants needed for witnesses and/or victims;
d. network with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of victims and cooperate with witness and victim protection institutions, social protection houses or trauma centers owned by the government, community or other institutions for health recovery of victims;
e. provide convenience, comfort, and safety for witnesses and/or victims;
f. maintain confidentiality of witnesses and/or victims;
g. provide fulfillment of legal assistance for witnesses and/or victims; and
h. in the event that integrated service delivery is carried out in a networked manner, be responsible for the entire referral process of services needed by victims.
(4) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) has the duties to: a. provide integrated services to victims of violence; and b. provide protection to victims of violence, reporters and witnesses.
(5) The provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (4) letter a is implemented in accordance with Standard Operating Procedures established by the Regional Government.
(6) The appointment of P2TP2A management as referred to in paragraph (1) is stipulated by Regent Decree.

Article 22
SPM as referred to in Article 21 has performance indicators including:
a. coverage of Women and Children Victims of Violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers in integrated service units;
b. coverage of Women and Children Victims of Violence who receive health services by trained health workers at Community Health Centers and Hospitals;
c. coverage of Social Rehabilitation services provided by trained Social Rehabilitation officers for Women and Children Victims of Violence in integrated service units;
d. coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for Women and Children Victims of Violence in integrated service units;
e. coverage of law enforcement from the investigation level up to court decisions on cases of Violence against Women and Children;
f. coverage of Women and Children Victims of Violence who receive legal aid services;
g. coverage of Repatriation services for Women and Children Victims of Violence; and
h. coverage of social reintegration services for Women and Children Victims of Violence.

Article 17
(1) The Regent coordinates the protection of women and children from acts of violence with:
a. the government;
b. the provincial government;
c. vertical agencies;
d. educational institutions; and
e. community institutions.
(2) Coordination as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. religious affairs;
b. health affairs;
c. education affairs;
d. social affairs;
e. legal affairs; and
f. employment affairs.

REGENT OF BULELENG,

Considering : a. that every citizen has the right to obtain a sense of security and freedom from all forms of violence that degrades human dignity;
b. that women and children are vulnerable groups who tend to experience violence, thus need to receive optimal protection;
c. that based on the provisions of Article 12 paragraph (2) letter b of Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government as has been amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government, women's empowerment and child protection constitute mandatory government affairs for regional governments;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Protection of Women and Children From Acts of Violence;

